{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016pAWdw2ZoiS5UagmDT5BMA","type":"tool_result","content":"36-whose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n37-(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\n38-argument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\n39:your own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n40-\n41-Scoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\n42-The exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n43-`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n44-\n45-## What you are optimising\n46-\n47-**CRPS is the objective. Lower is better.** It is the only quantity that measures your model\n48-directly: it compares your predicted distribution against what actually happened, prompt by\n49-prompt, and it does not depend on what anyone else submitted.\n50-\n51-A field-relative position is also reported, and it is tempting to optimise because it looks like a\n52-result. **Do not.** The submissions are tightly packed, and the per-prompt score the aggregation\n53-consumes is your CRPS *minus the lowest CRPS on that prompt* — which re-bases a small difference\n54-onto a much smaller number. A tenth of a percent of CRPS can therefore move a position by a\n55-hundred places in either direction. That makes position a loud, unstable readout of a tiny change:\n56-useful for checking you are in the right regime, useless as a target. Two runs of the same model\n57-can land far apart on it without differing in any way that matters.\n58-\n59-So: **judge every experiment on CRPS**, per configuration and per asset. Report position if you\n60-like, but never trade a CRPS regression for a better one.\n61-\n62-How the reported position is formed, for completeness: the aggregation does not average across\n63-assets. It weights each asset by `ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` (`synth.validator.moving_average`),\n64-normalizes per miner, then applies a softmax, so a high-coefficient asset carries much more\n65:weight. Your champion is measured over the **10 days following your data cutoff**, with each of\n66-the three configurations contributing one third regardless of asset count. The moving average\n67-backfills a model that appears late with the worst value in the set, so generation starts one\n68-horizon before the window and yours is never treated as late.\n69-\n70-## Your resources\n71-\n72-- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n73-  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n74-  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n75-  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n76-  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n77-  `agent/predict.py --days N --eval-end D` answers the prompts the validator scored in the N days\n78-  before D, at the start times it actually used, read from the offline field bundle: the same\n79:  kind of prompts your champion is scored on. `--eval-end` may be any day up to your cutoff, and\n80-  each run scores only its own predictions, so runs can go in parallel. It is also used for the 1h\n81-  format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment 60`). `--cadence-minutes N` answers an even\n82-  grid instead; a cadence that divides an hour puts every prompt on the round hour, where\n83-  volatility spikes, so do not read an absolute calibration level off such a grid.\n84-- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n85-  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n86-  trade_count`, resolve it with\n87-  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n88-  **2025-08-01 → 2026-10-11**, mounted\n89-  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n90-  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n91-  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n92-\n93-  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n94-  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n95-  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n96-\n97-  | From | Assets |\n98-  |---|---|\n99-  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n100-  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n101-  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n102-  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n103-\n104-  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n--\n180-  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n181-  evidence, that they don't improve on closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n182-  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n183:  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n184-  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n185-- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n186:  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n187-  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n188-  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n189-  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n190-- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n191-  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n192-  rules).\n193-\n194-## Your envelope\n195-\n196-- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n197-- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n198-  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n199-- Hardware: GCP n1-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 60GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA T4 (16GB VRAM; Turing, no bf16). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fdbca538-ca84-498b-80c3-b8a33e6553ce","uuid":"7ce0e19c-5855-4296-8272-fcf3c17a03af","timestamp":"2026-10-21T16:04:52.145Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"36-whose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n37-(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\n38-argument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\n39:your own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n40-\n41-Scoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\n42-The exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n43-`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n44-\n45-## What you are optimising\n46-\n47-**CRPS is the objective. Lower is better.** It is the only quantity that measures your model\n48-directly: it compares your predicted distribution against what actually happened, prompt by\n49-prompt, and it does not depend on what anyone else submitted.\n50-\n51-A field-relative position is also reported, and it is tempting to optimise because it looks like a\n52-result. **Do not.** The submissions are tightly packed, and the per-prompt score the aggregation\n53-consumes is your CRPS *minus the lowest CRPS on that prompt* — which re-bases a small difference\n54-onto a much smaller number. A tenth of a percent of CRPS can therefore move a position by a\n55-hundred places in either direction. That makes position a loud, unstable readout of a tiny change:\n56-useful for checking you are in the right regime, useless as a target. Two runs of the same model\n57-can land far apart on it without differing in any way that matters.\n58-\n59-So: **judge every experiment on CRPS**, per configuration and per asset. Report position if you\n60-like, but never trade a CRPS regression for a better one.\n61-\n62-How the reported position is formed, for completeness: the aggregation does not average across\n63-assets. It weights each asset by `ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` (`synth.validator.moving_average`),\n64-normalizes per miner, then applies a softmax, so a high-coefficient asset carries much more\n65:weight. Your champion is measured over the **10 days following your data cutoff**, with each of\n66-the three configurations contributing one third regardless of asset count. The moving average\n67-backfills a model that appears late with the worst value in the set, so generation starts one\n68-horizon before the window and yours is never treated as late.\n69-\n70-## Your resources\n71-\n72-- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n73-  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n74-  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n75-  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n76-  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n77-  `agent/predict.py --days N --eval-end D` answers the prompts the validator scored in the N days\n78-  before D, at the start times it actually used, read from the offline field bundle: the same\n79:  kind of prompts your champion is scored on. `--eval-end` may be any day up to your cutoff, and\n80-  each run scores only its own predictions, so runs can go in parallel. It is also used for the 1h\n81-  format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment 60`). `--cadence-minutes N` answers an even\n82-  grid instead; a cadence that divides an hour puts every prompt on the round hour, where\n83-  volatility spikes, so do not read an absolute calibration level off such a grid.\n84-- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n85-  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n86-  trade_count`, resolve it with\n87-  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n88-  **2025-08-01 → 2026-10-11**, mounted\n89-  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n90-  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n91-  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n92-\n93-  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n94-  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n95-  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n96-\n97-  | From | Assets |\n98-  |---|---|\n99-  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n100-  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n101-  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n102-  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n103-\n104-  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n--\n180-  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n181-  evidence, that they don't improve on closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n182-  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n183:  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n184-  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n185-- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n186:  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n187-  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n188-  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n189-  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n190-- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n191-  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n192-  rules).\n193-\n194-## Your envelope\n195-\n196-- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n197-- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n198-  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n199-- Hardware: GCP n1-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 60GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA T4 (16GB VRAM; Turing, no bf16). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
